T1  - Are Colombian sickness funds cream skimming enrollees? An analysis with suggestions for policy improvement.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1002/pam.20052
A1  - Trujillo, Antonio J
A1  - McCalla, Dawn C
Y1  - 2004//
N2  - One of the primary objectives of Colombian social health insurance reform was to increase competition among for-profit insurers. Unfortunately, the flat capitated formula creates an opportunity for sickness funds to maximize reimbursement gains by cream skimming--selecting against unhealthy individuals. This paper explores sickness fund selection behavior to evaluate the efficiency losses associated with the introduction of managed competition in Colombia. Data from a 1997 Colombian household survey are analyzed with a bivariate probit model with partial observability using instrumental variables. The model yields some evidence of sickness fund selection based on health status. Public policy options to discourage risk selection by health status are discussed.
